WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.230
RACHEL MYERS: Hi, I'm Rachel.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:12.268
I'm a software engineer on
Firebase Security Rules.

00:00:12.268 --> 00:00:13.810
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
And I'm Sebastian.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.760
And I work on Firestore.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.030
Rachel, do you still remember
what happened last May?

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:20.470
RACHEL MYERS: How
could I forget?

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.900
That was the month that
I was flooded with emails

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.570
from every website I
had ever signed up for.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.880
I looked at that flood
and I wondered why I ever

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.020
signed up for so many things.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.530
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: Last May,
the General Data Protection

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.890
Regulations took effect.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.340
This legislation touches
on pretty much every aspect

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.230
of user data collection.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.800
Among other things,
it regulates how

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.200
app developers are able to use,
collect, and store user data.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.810
It was one of the biggest
changes to user privacy

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.470
that happened in a while but
the basics of user privacy

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.280
remained the same.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.850
If you're building an app
that handles user data,

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:04.190
user privacy should be
one of your top concerns.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.300
Today, Rachel and I will
show you some of the tools

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.520
that we have built
at Firebase to help

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.940
you respect your user's data.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:15.940
Since neither of us are lawyers,
we cannot give you legal

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.020
advice.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.860
Instead, we are going to treat
user privacy like software

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:26.350
developers and stick to what
we know best as engineers.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.180
We will tell you
how Firebase can

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.520
help you with your
users' privacy

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:34.570
and share our personal
suggestions along the way.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.480
By not just focusing
on GDPR, we hope

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.460
that what you will
learn today will

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.425
be applicable to
all of your users.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.130
After all, user privacy
is larger than any one

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.200
piece of legislation.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.910
Even before last May, different
regulations around user privacy

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.180
applied to users
in most countries.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:02.350
Rachel and I don't know what
the future of data privacy

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.300
will look like.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.680
Instead, we will talk about the
Firebase tools and principles

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.470
that you can already use
today to build products

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.630
that put your users in control.

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:19.320
But first, let us
explain what Firebase is.

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.840
We built Firebase so
that app developers

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.800
can focus on the features
that make their app unique.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.890
We don't want you to have to
spend your time spinning up

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.390
a backend service just so that
you can store a bit of data.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.120
Firebase takes mundane tasks
out of building an app.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.410
We want to be with you from
your very first line of code

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:44.460
until you reach planet scale.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.250
Maybe you've heard
of Firebase before.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:48.950
It's OK if you haven't.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:53.430
We shipped our first product
in 2012, the Firebase RealTime

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.690
Database.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.510
Since then, we've been
growing our offerings

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.250
and have been watching
what customers

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.920
are doing with our products.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.210
In 2016, we introduced
the Firebase SDKs

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.920
for Cloud Storage.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.020
Cloud Storage for
Firebase allows

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.620
you to build apps that
let your users securely up

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.440
and download files
from Google's cloud.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.640
We gracefully handle
poor network connectivity

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.160
and use Cloud
Storage's excaching

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.810
to offer superior performance.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:33.160
And in 2017, we introduced
Cloud Functions for Firebase.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.440
Cloud Functions
for Firebase allows

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.560
you to run your own server
site code without forcing

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.200
you to setup your own servers.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:44.740
Cloud Function uses event-driven
lightweight JavaScript

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:47.200
that runs when data on
the backend changes.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.800
And just last year, we
launched Cloud Firestore.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.490
Clouds Firestore is a scalable
database for mobile web

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.440
and server development.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:03.950
We offer feature rich SDKs
that integrate directly

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.530
with the Firebase platform.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.740
Our SDKs support direct
from mobile access

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.710
and allow you to write
customer facing apps

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.735
without the need for
application servers.

00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:20.510
Today, Firebase
offers 17 products

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:24.450
that help you develop, grow,
and earn from your mobile apps.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:28.070
All of our products are built
around one core principle.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.490
We want you to build apps
with rich backend integrations

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:35.933
without having to set up
these backends yourself.

00:04:35.933 --> 00:04:38.100
RACHEL MYERS: We're in the
business of storing data.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.670
So naturally, we've put a lot
of thought into a question

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:45.200
that you or your CIO
might have asked recently.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.910
Do we handle those
privacy requirements

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.410
when we're building
Serverless apps?

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.810
User privacy was traditionally
not a concern for developers

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.147
when they were evaluating
Serverless backends.

00:04:57.147 --> 00:04:58.980
The questions that you
would use when you're

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.570
evaluating Serverless
products were

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:03.780
things like what features
does this platform offer?

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.510
How will the parts of
this interact together?

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:10.530
And how well will each
component scale up for me?

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.327
By contrast, the
concerns that you

00:05:12.327 --> 00:05:14.160
have when you're thinking
about user privacy

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.540
are pretty different.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:18.000
Those are things like
how do we make sure

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.160
that my data is secretly stored?

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:22.620
How will I make sure that
my users understand the data

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:23.872
that I'm collecting?

00:05:23.872 --> 00:05:25.830
And how will I make sure
that I remove the data

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:29.070
when I no longer need it?

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.290
So it doesn't seem like
there's a lot of overlap

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.240
between these two
types of questions.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:36.180
Some people have even suggested
that Serverless architecture

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:39.840
and data privacy could
even contradict each other.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.390
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: Seems like
Rachel is reading my mind.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.550
RACHEL MYERS: Or like we
wrote the talk together.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.180
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
But let's be honest,

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.990
even though I deeply
care about user privacy,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.170
I am not willing to give up
Serverless development just

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.550
yet.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.980
I don't want to go back
to the days of installing,

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.730
maintaining, and upgrading
my very own MySQL instances

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.760
just so that my Android app
can receive a single push

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.130
notification.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:08.730
Fortunately, Serverless design
patterns and user privacy

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.890
can often go hand in hand.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:14.640
At Firebase, we are proud to
offer products and tools that

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.300
help you protect
your users' data,

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.420
not despite, but because of,
our Serverless offerings.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:25.633
Let's dive in and take a
look at a few examples.

00:06:25.633 --> 00:06:27.300
RACHEL MYERS: We'll
start by showing you

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.790
how you can reduce the
data that you're storing.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.890
And you can use
Firebase's Cloud Functions

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.110
to make sure that you're
managing this data well.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.840
We'll show you examples of user
data deletion and user data

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.515
export.

00:06:40.515 --> 00:06:41.840
Hit the clicker.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.490
Sebastian and I
will dive into how

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.740
you can use Firebase's
security rules to limit

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.380
the access that people
have to your data

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.020
and how you can also
describe data ownership.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:53.420
And we'll discuss notifying
your users about the data

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:54.440
that you are collecting.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.380
That includes covering the
storage retention policies

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:58.532
for our backend.

00:06:58.532 --> 00:06:59.990
And we'll show you
patterns for how

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:02.150
you can store those privacy
preferences once users

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:03.530
have given them to you.

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:05.480
And we'll end with
some advanced use cases

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:08.435
like activity logs to track
changes to privacy settings.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.520
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
Rachel and I want

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.190
you to design your apps
for privacy from the very

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:17.810
first line of code.

00:07:17.810 --> 00:07:19.880
The easiest way to do
this is, of course,

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.560
to not store any data.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:24.123
RACHEL MYERS: Sebastien,
not store any data?

00:07:24.123 --> 00:07:25.040
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: OK.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.120
Let's stay realistic and only
save the data that we need.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.480
For example, let's
say we have an app

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:34.460
and we want to make
sure that our users are

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:38.060
European citizens over 18.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:40.880
Instead of storing the user's
full birthday and country

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:43.340
of birth, we only need
to store, whether they

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:46.390
meet our two criteria.

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.280
As you design your app,
think about what data

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.310
you need for it to function
and what the amount of time

00:07:52.310 --> 00:07:55.760
is that you need to
store this data for.

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:56.510
RACHEL MYERS: Woo.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:57.530
Thanks.

00:07:57.530 --> 00:07:59.910
Not everyone is starting
with a brand new project.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.490
So if you have an
existing project,

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:02.960
you're not out of luck.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:04.550
Let's look at some
privacy features

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:08.720
that we've added to an existing
app that we call Friendly Pics.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.940
Friendly Pics is a
reference implementation

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.700
for many different features
implemented on Firebase.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.640
And now, it includes
user privacy features.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:19.940
It has a straightforward
user facing feature set.

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:23.090
It lets users sign
in, upload photos.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:24.590
And then it has
some social features

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:27.020
like commenting and
favoriting photos.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.850
The data that's stored
by Friendly Pics

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:31.400
all supports one of
these three features.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.460
That stores account
information--

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.030
these are things that are in
addition to authentication data

00:08:38.030 --> 00:08:39.289
that we get.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:41.090
Next, Friendly Pics
stores the pictures

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:42.740
that users have uploaded.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:44.430
A normal app might
keep this forever,

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:46.040
but for Friendly
Pics because we want

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.600
to show lots of
privacy features,

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.417
we delete all of the
photos after one month.

00:08:51.417 --> 00:08:53.750
And finally, Friendly Pics
saves the social interactions

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:54.830
with these pictures.

00:08:54.830 --> 00:08:57.050
These are things like
likes and comments.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:09:00.530
And we keep them until those
things are deleted by the users

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:05.480
or until the photo it's
associated with is deleted.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.190
So whether you're
writing a new app

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.220
or you're improving the privacy
of an existing application,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.990
the first step to
managing your privacy

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:14.570
is to understand exactly
what data you're storing

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:16.690
and the features that
that data is supporting.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.440
That will give you an
idea of how long you

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:19.940
need to support it.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.790
It gives you a position to
start scripting the data removal

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:24.650
once you no longer need it.

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:28.070
So many organizations want to
limit the data they're storing.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:29.990
And they only want to
keep the data they need.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:32.480
And they only want to keep it
for as long as they need it.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.510
Those are some
principles to live by.

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.550
And I want to stop
here and acknowledge

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:41.270
that it isn't always as easy as
you might think to delete data.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.100
You might have a great
engineering proposal

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.947
for how you're going to remove
the data you no longer need.

00:09:45.947 --> 00:09:47.780
And that could meet
with a lot of resistance

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.198
within the organization.

00:09:49.198 --> 00:09:50.990
Maybe you have someone
in the org who hates

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:53.250
the idea of data going away.

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:55.340
For example, you have
an analytics person

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:57.650
that likes to run a
query for how many users

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:03.210
between the ages of 18-25 are
performing a particular action.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.060
So you could run
that query, if you

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.520
know that they're doing this,
and save that daily count

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.360
on the number of users.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.900
And then you can delete
the data associated

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:12.933
with individual users.

00:10:12.933 --> 00:10:15.350
That way you keep your analytics
person happy and you also

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.130
keep your users happy.

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.040
So when you're auditing
the data that you

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.070
need across your organization,
be as specific as possible.

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:24.290
If you only need count
of the number of users,

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:27.512
make sure that you know that.

00:10:27.512 --> 00:10:29.970
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: Even if we
store only the minimum amount

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:33.300
of data, we should
still structure it

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:37.080
in a way that makes it
easy to delete it later.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.810
To me, offering
users the ability

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:42.180
to delete their own data
is an important pillar

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.300
of user privacy.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.160
You should delete
user data not only

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.210
when a user requests that
their account is being removed

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:54.180
but also when you no longer
need a specific set of data

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:56.520
for your app to function.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.610
If you design your app
with user privacy in mind,

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:03.850
deleting user data can be
pretty simple with Firebase.

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.060
The asynchronous
nature of most deletes

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.080
makes deleting a great use case
for Google Cloud Functions,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.790
a service that
should already be one

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:16.470
of the primary tools in
your Serverless tool belt.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.530
While deletion is often
triggered by explicit actions

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:23.710
in your app-- as in this example
of a user deleting their post--

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:26.700
remember that you ultimately
don't control the requests that

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:29.920
come from your client.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.370
You don't want to rely
solely on your customers

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.980
to clean up their own data.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.530
To remove data that
is no longer needed,

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:39.780
you can instead create
a cloud function

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.360
that cleans up unneeded items.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.110
RACHEL MYERS: Cloud
Functions for Firebase

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:47.360
are like programmatic glue.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.540
It lets you extend and connect
all of the Firebase products

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.610
and all of the Cloud
products as well.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:55.550
So you can create a completely
Serverless backend for your web

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:57.530
and your mobile applications.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:59.598
Cloud Functions
are event driven.

00:11:59.598 --> 00:12:00.890
They're lightweight JavaScript.

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:02.860
And this means that
they are deployed--

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:05.330
and this means that
when something happens,

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.070
it causes this function to run.

00:12:07.070 --> 00:12:09.420
That's the way to
think about it.

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:11.270
So how do functions work?

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:14.270
All Cloud Functions are
triggered by some action

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:16.050
that is taken on your system.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.800
This could be something
like an image is uploaded

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:21.203
or a user has
clicked this button.

00:12:21.203 --> 00:12:23.620
In this case, let's say we
want to trigger a function when

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:25.400
a user logs in.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:28.130
When that event occurs,
an event is emitted--

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:31.820
in this case from
Firebase authentication.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:35.240
A new environment is spun
up that only has my code

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.820
and the dependencies that
I've declared for my code.

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:40.610
Then that code within
my function is executed.

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:43.430
In this example, say
when the user logs in,

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.470
my function will create
some default records

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:48.680
for the user in Firestore.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.840
As another example, when
you delete a document

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:53.180
from Firestore, you can
trigger a custom function

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:56.485
that can cleanup all
of that related data.

00:12:56.485 --> 00:12:57.860
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
Sometimes it's

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.160
obvious when data is obsolete.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.960
In Friendly Pics, for example,
we delete all comments

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:06.530
and likes when the user requests
that we delete a picture.

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:09.830
For other use cases,
it's not as apparent.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:13.040
If your app has functionality
that only temporarily needs

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.120
some of its data, I'd recommend
deleting the unused items when

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:20.150
they are no longer needed.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:23.390
An example of a case where
you only temporarily need data

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:25.670
is a high score list
in which you only

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:29.525
need to keep the highest scores
and not the complete history.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.420
Friendly Pics really
is a good model here.

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.420
It doesn't keep any
data longer than 30 days

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:40.300
and uses a Cloud Function
to periodically delete

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.940
older items.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.310
You can find an
article that explains

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:47.470
how to set up a cron
job to trigger a Cloud

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:49.720
Function at the
short link or you

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.000
can use the new Cloud
Scheduler to schedule

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.683
functions to run periodically.

00:13:54.683 --> 00:13:56.350
RACHEL MYERS: You'll
also want to ensure

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:58.600
that you no longer
keep any user data when

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:00.880
users delete their accounts.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.800
If using Firebase
Authentication,

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:07.830
you can call the delete API
from the Firebase User Object

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:09.620
to delete all of
that information.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:12.640
But calling delete will not,
however, remove user data

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:16.180
that's stored in other
products, for example Firestore.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.950
If you're going
Serverless, that cleanup

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:20.800
is a great use case
for Firebase Functions.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.260
The function could either be
triggered by an authentication

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:24.310
delete event.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:28.010
Or if you're not using
Firebase Authentication,

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:31.305
you can trigger the function
via an HTTP request.

00:14:31.305 --> 00:14:33.430
We've done some of the work
to get you started here

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:35.770
by publishing a general
purpose account deletion

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.450
function that you
can tune to work

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:39.230
with exactly your data model.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:40.850
So let's walk
through that quickly.

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:42.460
First, this is my function.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:45.120
It lives in index.js.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:47.470
When a user clicks the
delete account button,

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:51.820
that's going to call the delete
API in Firebase Authentication.

00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:54.520
And that triggers
this function to run.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.560
But my code is generalized here.

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:58.690
To make it work specifically
for this one app,

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:02.920
I needed to add a JSON file
called userprivacy.JSON that

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.960
lives in the Functions folder.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.940
This file allows me to
delete the paths that

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:09.520
need to be removed when a
user deletes their accounts.

00:15:12.088 --> 00:15:14.380
I can define the paths for
all the services that I use,

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:18.090
such as Firestore, Realtime
Database, and storage.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:20.410
And I'm using a variable
here for the parts

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:23.710
of that that will be
replaced by the user's ID.

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:26.020
When the function is
triggered, I get the UID

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:27.975
from the event.dataobject.

00:15:27.975 --> 00:15:29.350
And because I went
to remove data

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:32.230
from Firestore, the Realtime
Database, and storage,

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.450
I'm going to call
three helper functions.

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:36.310
If you're not using
one of these services,

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:38.320
you can easily
exclude that and not

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.540
include that unused function.

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:43.270
Each of the helper functions
share the same basic structure.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:45.730
But let's walk through the
function from the Realtime

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:47.920
Database as an example.

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:51.880
First, I'm going to fetch the
relevant paths from the JSON

00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:52.637
file.

00:15:52.637 --> 00:15:54.970
And then I'm going to iterate
through each one of those,

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:57.490
removing the data at that path.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.830
When each iteration returns,
I'm going to return control

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:01.030
to the top-level function.

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:02.100
Can I throw it to you?

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:03.044
Oh, OK.

00:16:08.197 --> 00:16:10.780
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: You may also
want to offer your user's data

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:12.610
portability.

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:14.830
Providing users a
copy of their data

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.440
in a well-known and
machine readable format

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.440
allows them to transfer their
data between different service

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:23.050
providers.

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:26.830
We've released another Cloud
Function called Export Data

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:29.830
that can help you with this.

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:33.190
The Export Data function is
triggered by an HTTP request

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:38.530
that you can make when the user
requests a copy of their data.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.930
It calls three helper
functions that perform

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.090
the export for each product.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:44.870
The helper function--
in this case,

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.040
the one for the
Realtime Database--

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:49.300
use the same user
privacy, the JSON

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:51.520
file that Rachel
just showed you.

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:54.260
You can use the exact
same configuration

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:57.550
or you can provide
separate path.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:01.450
The helper functions
iterate through the schema

00:17:01.450 --> 00:17:04.945
and exported JSON file that is
then uploaded to Cloud Storage.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.730
It is incredibly
important that this

00:17:09.730 --> 00:17:11.710
exported data in
Cloud Storage is

00:17:11.710 --> 00:17:16.900
only accessible to the user
whose data you export it.

00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:19.750
You can accomplish this
by using Firebase Security

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:25.089
Rules, the security model for
all the Firebase's products.

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:28.690
Since the export data function
uploads the exported data

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:32.500
to a location that includes
the user's user ID,

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.570
you can create a
security rule like this

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:37.270
which makes the data only
accessible to that user.

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:43.600
Remember, that it is essential
that you audit your security

00:17:43.600 --> 00:17:47.110
rules if you're storing any
user data with Firebase.

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.450
With Firebase
Security Rules, if you

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:52.060
deny access in one
rule's expression

00:17:52.060 --> 00:17:55.030
but grant access in
another, we combine them

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.590
and access is granted.

00:17:57.590 --> 00:18:00.130
Make sure that you
understand that our rules are

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:00.830
ord together.

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.850
RACHEL MYERS: If you have a
more complicated data model that

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:09.060
can't be expressed as a list
of wildcard paths like the one

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:11.707
we have in our JSON
file, that's OK.

00:18:11.707 --> 00:18:13.290
In that case, I would
suggest that you

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:16.500
maintain a list of locations
for the user's data.

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.080
And you can use that list as
the input for a similar Cloud

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:20.550
Function that,
just like this one,

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:23.700
iterates through those paths
and either deletes or copies

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.310
that data over,
depending on if you're

00:18:25.310 --> 00:18:26.910
doing an export or a delete.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:33.340
If you keep data
portability in mind

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:36.100
as you are building your app,
then deleting and exporting

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.020
data can be as
simple as configuring

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:40.210
one of these general
purpose functions.

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:44.200
Serverless development
can respect user privacy.

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:46.240
And we're making it
easier than ever.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:47.710
With Cloud Functions
for Firebase,

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:49.877
you can manage your user's
data without building out

00:18:49.877 --> 00:18:53.022
expensive infrastructure.

00:18:53.022 --> 00:18:55.230
And we've published a guide
for clearing and removing

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:55.950
user's data.

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.900
You can find more
about it at this link.

00:19:01.202 --> 00:19:02.660
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
Let's recall how

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:06.050
security is handled in
the traditional backend

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:07.820
application.

00:19:07.820 --> 00:19:11.600
In a, let's call it,
serverfull application,

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:14.240
there is a client,
a storage layer,

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:17.360
and an application layer.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:19.190
The application
layer is often used

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:22.610
to enforce security
and access controls.

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:25.160
If a client sends a
malicious request,

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:28.880
the application layer
can drop the request.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:31.580
In Serverless systems,
the application layer

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:33.240
has been removed.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:36.690
The client connects directly
to the storage layer.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.500
So we ask for the
Serverless system and force

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:42.810
access controls.

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:46.230
In Firebase, access
controls are enforced right

00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:48.120
at the storage layer.

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.640
We validate every
incoming request

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:53.550
against the security
rules that you configured.

00:19:56.350 --> 00:19:58.690
Security rules are
your first line

00:19:58.690 --> 00:20:02.380
of defense in creating
secure Firebase apps.

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:05.500
And secure apps really
are an essential part

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.680
of treating your
user's data well.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:10.350
And correctly configuring
security rules

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:13.810
is an important step in
preventing unauthorized data

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:15.160
access.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:17.410
RACHEL MYERS: We've heard
from a lot of Firebase users

00:20:17.410 --> 00:20:20.570
that security rules can be
intimidating or even confusing.

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:23.178
So we've invested a lot
of time, and energy,

00:20:23.178 --> 00:20:24.970
and thought into creating
new products that

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:27.340
make it easier than ever.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:29.638
So because security
rules are so important,

00:20:29.638 --> 00:20:31.180
we want to spend a
little bit of time

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.388
in this talk walking through
some of the new features

00:20:33.388 --> 00:20:36.700
that we've built
around security rules.

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:38.830
First, before you
deploy your rules,

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.690
you can try them
out and play around

00:20:40.690 --> 00:20:44.220
with what you might want that
to look like in the Web Console.

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:45.460
There are simulators.

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.110
And they let you test
different types of requests.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:51.362
So you can say for this type
of request, should it succeed?

00:20:51.362 --> 00:20:53.320
And then you can test to
make sure it succeeds.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:54.820
For a different
type of request, you

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.460
can test that it does fail
just like you want it to.

00:20:57.460 --> 00:20:59.330
You can block access.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.390
The simulators, though,
have a limitation.

00:21:01.390 --> 00:21:04.390
They only help you as
you're writing the test,

00:21:04.390 --> 00:21:06.550
as you're writing
your security rules.

00:21:06.550 --> 00:21:10.270
What happens if last week,
Sebastian wrote great security

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:13.383
rules, and this week I
came around and tried

00:21:13.383 --> 00:21:14.800
to improve them
and I accidentally

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:18.728
granted access to a lot of
people that I shouldn't have?

00:21:18.728 --> 00:21:21.270
To make sure that you can have
confidence in your rules going

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:25.740
forward, we now offer a Security
Rules Emulator, simulator

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:28.140
and emulator, small
difference in words,

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:29.910
big difference in product.

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:31.900
And then the
emulators run locally.

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:34.140
So you can run this locally.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:36.468
You can write tests
around what your rules do.

00:21:36.468 --> 00:21:38.010
And then you can
include that as part

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:40.110
of your continuous
integration workflow.

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:40.980
They're npm tests.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:42.397
You can include
them really easily

00:21:42.397 --> 00:21:43.930
in what you're already running.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:46.561
So check out the link
for more Information.

00:21:49.488 --> 00:21:51.030
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
The security rules

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:54.270
we've shown you today describe
a schema for your data

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:56.940
and spell out invariance
for your backend.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:22:00.610
They allow you to limit not
only who can access your data,

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:06.390
but also limit how the data
itself relates to one another.

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:10.950
Please remember to never trust
the data from your client apps.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:13.440
Since you no longer have
an application layer,

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:18.420
security rules are the best
way to enforce access controls.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:21.990
Use the Security Rules Simulator
we offer in our Firebase Web

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:26.310
Console to help you write and
verify your security rules.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:30.000
And use the new
Security Rules Emulator

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.405
to create automatic
tests around your rules.

00:22:34.835 --> 00:22:36.460
RACHEL MYERS: Now,
we're ready to start

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:39.800
informing our users about the
data that will be collected.

00:22:39.800 --> 00:22:42.730
At Firebase, we've published
a guide to our data retention

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:43.900
on our website.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:45.400
And you can include
this information

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:47.260
as you inform your
users about what you're

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:50.720
doing with data collection.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:54.140
The Firebase Data Privacy Terms
also explain what kind of data

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:57.650
each Firebase product collects,
how long that data is retained,

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:01.490
and how you can
delete that data.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:03.140
And we've also
released a white paper

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:05.630
that explains what happened
inside of Google Cloud

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:08.870
when you invoke an
API to delete data.

00:23:08.870 --> 00:23:10.410
This is more for
your information,

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.090
if you're curious than for
your data collection policies.

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:15.590
But if you'd like to see that,
it's available on the website

00:23:15.590 --> 00:23:16.980
as well.

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:17.480
Thank you.

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:24.360
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: When you
read our data privacy terms,

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.840
you may see that some Firebase
products like Cloud Messaging

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:33.250
or AB Testing have the ability
to identify individual devices.

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:35.670
This device level
identification is

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:38.460
done via Firebase Instance ID.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:41.520
And it allows us to message
or change the experience

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:44.730
for only a subset of your users.

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:47.970
Because Firebase Instance
IDs are so important,

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:51.690
let's quickly go over them.

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:54.840
If you incorporate a product
that uses Firebase Instance

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:58.650
IDs, Firebase automatically
assigns a unique ID

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:00.930
to each app installation.

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:03.030
If you install two
different Firebase

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:06.560
apps on the same device, they
each have a different Instance

00:24:06.560 --> 00:24:07.590
ID.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:11.010
If you install the same app
on two different devices,

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:14.010
say an iPhone and an
iPad, they will also

00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:16.680
have two different Instance IDs.

00:24:16.680 --> 00:24:20.400
On the other hand, when
you restart the same app

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:24.900
on the same device, Firebase
keeps the same Instance ID.

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:28.020
That's how we can send
notifications to apps even when

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:30.676
they are not open.

00:24:30.676 --> 00:24:33.240
While Instance ID
data is generally not

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.910
meant to be personally
identifying,

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:38.970
it can potentially be linked
to any personal profile data

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.580
that you store in
other products.

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.040
If you want to allow
your users to opt out

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:46.170
of this type of
data collection, you

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:48.600
can disable Instance
ID generation.

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:55.330
We have also added dedicated
APIs to our Admin SDKs

00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:59.000
that let you delete the
Instance IDs themselves.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:01.150
It is your responsibility
as a developer

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:06.400
to invoke these APIs when a
user requests account deletion.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.310
For Firebase products that
key data by instance ID,

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:13.780
at the time it takes to remove
this data varies by product.

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:15.580
And you can find
more information

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:18.965
on this in our privacy terms.

00:25:18.965 --> 00:25:20.840
RACHEL MYERS: Keep in
mind that deleting data

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:23.030
from our backend system
does not immediately

00:25:23.030 --> 00:25:24.930
delete all copies of that data.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:29.030
Some of it lives in backup
systems and caches temporarily.

00:25:29.030 --> 00:25:31.760
So you can look at the terms
to understand more about when

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:33.853
it will be completely deleted.

00:25:33.853 --> 00:25:35.270
So now that we
understand the data

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:36.890
that Google is
saving and retaining

00:25:36.890 --> 00:25:38.682
and you have in mind
the data that you need

00:25:38.682 --> 00:25:40.580
to support the features
that you've written,

00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:42.385
let's talk about
how to inform users

00:25:42.385 --> 00:25:43.760
and give them all
the information

00:25:43.760 --> 00:25:46.800
they need to make a
meaningful decision.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:49.700
In Friendly Pics,
we've added a notice

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.460
to let users know what
kind of data is being saved

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.830
and what features
that data supports.

00:25:54.830 --> 00:25:56.600
This notice gives them
a choice if they'd

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:58.910
like us to save that
data or if they'd

00:25:58.910 --> 00:26:00.730
like to disable that feature.

00:26:00.730 --> 00:26:02.750
And features are only
enabled for the users

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:04.880
that want that data saved.

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:06.770
They'd have to actively
check to make sure

00:26:06.770 --> 00:26:08.900
that that data will be saved.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:10.640
For example, if a
user doesn't want

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:12.470
us to share their
social data, we

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:15.260
remove the ability to
add comments altogether.

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:18.812
If the user doesn't want us
to store any data at all,

00:26:18.812 --> 00:26:21.020
Friendly Pics still has a
really good read-only mode.

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.500
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: Depending
on how much of your app's

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:28.570
functionality is
handled by our backends,

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:31.380
you may also need to
process the user's privacy

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:35.430
settings in Firestore or
in the Realtime Database.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:37.350
When you consider
structuring this data,

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:40.320
you should look at
how it will be read

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:44.247
and what privacy controls
your backend actually needs.

00:26:44.247 --> 00:26:46.830
You'll likely want to make sure
that these settings are stored

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:49.620
in a place where you can
prevent unauthorized access

00:26:49.620 --> 00:26:52.330
by other users.

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:56.190
For example, if your user's
profile information is public,

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:58.740
you probably want different
access restrictions

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:02.640
to these privacy
related settings.

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:05.260
You can use a layout
such as the one here

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:08.040
which contains a private
profile section in addition

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:11.630
to a public one.

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:14.570
Alternatively, you could store
their privacy preferences

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:19.693
in a separate section of
your database altogether.

00:27:19.693 --> 00:27:21.860
RACHEL MYERS: You also want
to build a way for users

00:27:21.860 --> 00:27:23.600
to update their
privacy settings.

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:26.060
This allows you to build
new features over time

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:28.538
or to modify existing features.

00:27:28.538 --> 00:27:31.080
It's one thing, however, to let
people update their settings.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:33.560
And it's another thing to think
through the process of what

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:36.290
you'll do with your data
when those settings change.

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:39.740
For example, say a user
grants consent for us

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:42.010
to save their photos and
then they change their mind.

00:27:42.010 --> 00:27:43.760
What should we do with
the existing photos

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:45.830
that we've already saved?

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:48.650
This balance between user
expectations and user privacy

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:50.670
is different in
every application.

00:27:50.670 --> 00:27:53.890
In Friendly Pics, we decided
to keep the existing pictures

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:55.790
in case the user wants
to see those still.

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:57.770
They can still delete
them on their own

00:27:57.770 --> 00:28:02.200
or we'll delete all of them
when they delete their account.

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:05.010
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT: As users
change their consent settings,

00:28:05.010 --> 00:28:07.650
you may also want to keep
a record of these changes

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:09.760
in an activity log.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:13.620
An activity log allows you
to share security or privacy

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:16.860
related changes with
your users and gives them

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:22.030
the ability to monitor
changes to their accounts.

00:28:22.030 --> 00:28:23.800
If you're storing
privacy settings

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:26.920
in Firestore or in
the Realtime Database,

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:30.610
this is another good case
for Google Cloud Functions.

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:33.520
With Cloud Functions,
Firebase can automatically

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:37.860
maintain an activity
log for you.

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:40.600
It's straightforward to
create a small function that

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:44.290
is triggered every time a
user's privacy settings change

00:28:44.290 --> 00:28:46.330
and which then copies
the new settings

00:28:46.330 --> 00:28:50.000
to an append only
storage location.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:52.540
Here's an example of
an activity logger

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:56.460
that listens to changes in the
privacy collection of Firestore

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:01.960
and adds entries to a dedicated
activity log collection.

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:04.810
If you want to make sure that
this activity log is append

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:08.320
only and cannot be modified,
you can use Security Rules

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:09.505
to prevent any changes.

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:15.830
For example, if your activity
log uses the Firestore

00:29:15.830 --> 00:29:18.470
collection for each
user and a document

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:21.050
for each time a user
changes their settings,

00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:24.680
you can use a rule like this
which allows document creation

00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:27.880
but doesn't allow
updates of existing data.

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:33.590
We've created a guide
to storing and using

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.900
privacy settings
in Firebase that

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.560
explains how to automatically
maintain activity logs.

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:43.610
You can find a link
to it on this slide

00:29:43.610 --> 00:29:46.648
or on the Firebase website.

00:29:46.648 --> 00:29:48.440
RACHEL MYERS: So building
privacy features,

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:51.500
like secure access
controls, data portability,

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:54.680
and data cleanup, can be within
easy reach of every developer

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:56.925
using Serverless architectures.

00:29:56.925 --> 00:29:59.300
Many of the privacy features
that we've pointed out today

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:02.180
can be easily deployed
to Cloud Functions.

00:30:02.180 --> 00:30:04.490
And we've shown how you
can lock down your data

00:30:04.490 --> 00:30:06.320
using Firebase Security Rules.

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:08.630
We hope that the tools and
technology we've highlighted

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:11.180
can help you turn user privacy
into some of the easiest

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:14.480
features that you
put on your app.

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:16.220
SEBASTIAN SCHMIDT:
GDPR is not the first

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:19.040
and it will certainly not be
the last piece of legislation

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:22.140
that changes how we
handle user data.

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:24.920
The ever growing list of
user privacy regulations

00:30:24.920 --> 00:30:26.780
can seem really daunting.

00:30:26.780 --> 00:30:32.240
And it makes touching any
kind of data really difficult.

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:34.220
But let's all take a
step back, and let's

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:38.780
instead focus on doing the
right thing for our users.

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:41.840
If we treat our users'
data with respect,

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:44.750
if we make sure our data
retention policies are

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:48.470
easy to understand, if we
limit the amount of data

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:52.670
that we handle, and if we
offer our users the ability

00:30:52.670 --> 00:30:55.160
to make meaningful
decisions about the data

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:59.180
that they lend to us, then
we'll likely be in a good place

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:01.910
no matter what privacy
regulations the future

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:04.640
may bring.

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:08.300
Come find us later at the
Firebase booth in the Expo.

00:31:08.300 --> 00:31:09.860
We'd love to talk
to you about how

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:13.290
you can be responsible
with your users' data.

00:31:13.290 --> 00:31:16.010
Remember that we
can't help us lawyers

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:18.155
but we can help you as friends.

00:31:18.155 --> 00:31:19.030
RACHEL MYERS: Thanks.

00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:21.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

